<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Waldburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Waldburg is a Swabian noble family named after Waldburg castle near Ravensburg. Its members served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors as imperial stewards (Truchsess) and later divided into several lines, among them Waldburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfegg">Wolfegg</a> and Waldburg-Zeil, which ruled small counties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-swabia">Upper Swabia</a>. Their lands were mediatised in 1806, mainly to Württemberg, and both lines survive as princely families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-waldburg.jpg" alt="House of Waldburg" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unbekannt / Vektorisiert von Flow2 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wappen_Waldburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The house, founded some time before the 12th century, traces its lines to Eberhard von Tanne-Waldburg, who died in 1234. He was steward and adviser to the Staufen dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and later to Emperor Friedrich II. In the anti-Staufen uprising he and his brother Friedrich von Tanne took opposite sides; after Friedrich was killed at Montefiascone in 1197, Eberhard was guardian of his nephew Heinrich until 1220, and the two then administered Swabia while the emperors were away. The imperial regalia were kept at Waldburg from 1220 to 1225.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1258 the stewards of Waldburg received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Alttrauchburg, which they bought outright in 1306 from its indebted count and enlarged with a large outer bailey and a strong tower. In 1429 it passed to the Jacobian line of the family, some of whose lords appear in the sources as bad stewards, always in financial difficulties. Damaged in various conflicts, the castle was strengthened in the 16th century into a seat of territorial lordship and government.</p><p>In 1628 the Waldburgs became imperial counts, and in 1690 they moved to their schloss at Kißlegg, using Trauchburg as a quarry for it. When the Jacobian line died out with Count Francis Charles Eusebius in 1772, its estate passed to the counts of Waldburg-Zeil, who still own the castle. The house divided into many comital lines, among them Waldburg-Wurzach, Waldburg-Scheer, Waldburg-Trauchburg, Waldburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfegg">Wolfegg</a> and Waldburg-Zeil, and as one of a small number of mediatised houses it belongs to the high nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Members of the house held high office. Conrad of Winterstetten, who died in 1243, was imperial butler, procurator of Swabia and guardian of Henry (VII) and Conrad IV. Georg Truchsess von Waldburg, known as Bauernjörg, was field captain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>; at Böblingen on 12 May 1525 he attacked a force of 15,000 armed peasants, of whom 3,000 were killed. Gebhard Truchsess von Waldburg was Archbishop and Prince-Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> from 1577 to 1588.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Waldburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…himself King Frederick I. Joining the Confederation of the Rhine in July 1806, he absorbed the lands of mediatised counts and princes such as the Hohenlohe, <b>Waldburg</b> and Königsegg families, the Teutonic Order's territory around Mergentheim in 1809, and in 1810 the city of Ulm, received from Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of <b>Waldburg</b> near Ravensburg served the Hohenstaufen as imperial stewards (Truchsess).</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-waldburg">County of Waldburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The market beside the abbey became a town in the 13th century and passed to the <b>Truchsesses of Waldburg</b>, from whom it bought its freedom in 1365, becoming a free imperial city.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-isny">Free Imperial City of Isny</a></li></ul></section>
